                    The tropical hyperfield
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝕋
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{T}
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#the_triangle_hyperfield_"}
                    The triangle hyperfield
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Δ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_entries"} Related entries
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %strong hyperring
            is like a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            but not with an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/underlying"} underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
            but with an underlying
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+hypergroup"} canonical hypergroup
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hypermonoid"} hypermonoid
            with additional ring-like
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/addition"} addition
            in a hyperring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multi-valued+function"} multi-valued function
            %strong hyperfield
            is a hyperring where every nonzero element has a multiplicative inverse.
          %p
            From
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#Viro10"} Viro 2010
          %blockquote
            %p
              “Krasner, Marshall, Connes and Consani and the author came to hyperfields for different reasons, motivated by different mathematical problems, but we came to the same conclusion: the hyperrings and hyperfields are great, very useful and very underdeveloped in the mathematical literature… Probably, the main obstacle for hyperfields to become a mainstream notion is that a multivalued operation does not fit to the tradition of set-theoretic terminology, which forces to avoid multivalued maps at any cost. I believe the taboo on multivalued maps has no real ground, and eventually will be removed. Hyperfields, as well as multigroups, hyperrings and multirings, are legitimate algebraic objects related in many ways to the classical core of mathematics… I believe hyperfields are to displace the tropical semifield in the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tropical+geometry"} tropical geometry
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %strong hyperring
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+set"} non-empty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            equipped with
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                a
                %em hyper-addition
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi R
                      %mo ×
                      %mi R
                      %mo ⟶
                      %msup
                        %mi P
                        %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} + \,\colon\, R\times R \longrightarrow P^*(R)
                (where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi P
                        %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P^*(R)
                is the set of non-empty subsets)
            %li
              %p
                a
                %em
                  multiplication
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⋅
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mi R
                        %mo ×
                        %mi R
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdot \,\colon\, R \times R \longrightarrow R
            %li
              %p
                chosen
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elements"} elements
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ∈
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0,1 \,\in\, R
          %p such that
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi R
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (R,+)
                is a
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+hypergroup"} canonical hypergroup
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi R
                      %mo ,
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (R,\cdot)
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
                with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+element"} identity element
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi r
                      %mo ,
                      %mi s
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi R
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi r
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi s
                      %mo +
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi r
                      %mi s
                      %mo +
                      %mi r
                      %mi t
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall r,s \in R \,\colon\, r(s+t) = r s + r t
                and
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi s
                        %mo +
                        %mi t
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi r
                        %mo =
                        %mi s
                        %mi r
                        %mo +
                        %mi t
                        %mi r
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (s + t) r = s r + t r
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi r
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi R
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi r
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mn 0
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi r
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall r \in R \,\colon\, r \cdot 0 = 0 \cdot r = 0
                ;
          %p#ReferencesForTheDefinition
            (e.g.
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "Nakassis87"} Nakassis 1987 Def. 4
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#DavvazLeoreanu-Fotea07"} Davvaz &amp; Leoreanu-Fotea 2007 Def. 3.1.1
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ≠
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \neq 1
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#ConnesConsani11"} Connes &amp; Consani 2011 Def. 2.1
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Jun15"} Jun 2015 Def. 2.2
          %p
            A
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
              of hyperrings
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/map"} map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \,\colon\, R_1 \to R_2
            such that
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi R
                        %mn 1
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo +
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo +
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall a,b \in R_1 \,\colon\, f(a + b) \subset f(a) + f(b)
                ;
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi R
                        %mn 1
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall a,b\in R_1 \,\colon\, f(a \cdot b) = f(a) \cdot f(b)
                \.
          %p
            A
            %strong hyperfield
            is a hyperring for which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo ∖
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (R \setminus \{0\}, \cdot)
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ≠
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \ne 1
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#equivalence_classes_in_a_ring Equivalence classes in a ring
          %p
            We can form many examples of hyperrings by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+ring"} quotienting
            a ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            by some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subgroup"} subgroup
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo ⊆
                  %msup
                    %mi R
                    %mo ×
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \subseteq R^{\times}
            of its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+of+units"} group of units
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                  %mi G
                  %mo =
                  %mi G
                  %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r G = G r
            for all
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r \in R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R/G
            is given by an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+class"} equivalence class
            of elements of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∼
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \sim y
            if and only if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo =
                  %mi y
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x = y g
            for some
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \in G
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                    %mi G
                    %mo =
                    %mi G
                    %mi r
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r G = G r
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R/G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [x]
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [y]
            is the set of all equivalence classes
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi x
                    %mo +
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [x + y]
          %p
            In particular, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is a hyperfield so is
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R/G
          %p
            We say a hyperring is
            %em doubly distributive
            if obeys the familiar identity
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo +
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo +
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                  %mi c
                  %mo +
                  %mi a
                  %mi d
                  %mo +
                  %mi b
                  %mi c
                  %mo +
                  %mi b
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a + b)(c + d) = a c + a d + b c + b d
          %p Not every hyperring is doubly distributive (see the example of the triangle hyperring below): in general, we merely have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo +
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo +
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi a
                  %mi c
                  %mo +
                  %mi a
                  %mi d
                  %mo +
                  %mi b
                  %mi c
                  %mo +
                  %mi b
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a + b)(c + d) \subseteq a c + a d + b c + b d
          %p
            However, every hyperring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R/G
            obtained as a quotient of a ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            by the above construction is doubly distributive.
          %h3#the_krasner_hyperfield_
            The Krasner hyperfield
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕂
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{K}
          %p
            The
            %em Krasner hyperfield
            is the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕂
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{K} = \{0,1\}
            with multiplication given by
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \cdot x = 0
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \cdot x = x
          %p and with addition given by
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo +
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 + x = x
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1+1 = \{0,1\}
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕂
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{K}
            is the quotient of the field of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            by its multiplicative subgroup consisting of all nonzero numbers:
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            is the equivalence class containing
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            is the equivalence class containing all nonzero numbers. The hyper-addition law above encodes how such equivalence classes behave under addition: for example, the sum of two nonzero numbers can be zero or nonzero.
          %h3#the_sign_hyperfield_
            The sign hyperfield
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕊
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{S}
          %p
            The
            %em sign hyperfield
            is the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕊
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{S} = \{0,1,-1\}
            with multiplication given by
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \cdot x = 0
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \cdot x = 1
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -1 \cdot -1 = 1
          %p and with addition given by
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo +
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 + x = x
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1+1 = \{1\}
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo +
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -1 + -1 = \{-1\}
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo +
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 + -1 = \{-1, 0, 1\}
                \.
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕊
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{S}
            is the quotient of the field of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            by its multiplicative subgroup of positive numbers:
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            is the equivalence class containing the positive numbers,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            is the equivalence class containing
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -1
            as the equivalence class of the negative numbers. Again, the hyper-addition law encodes how such equivalence classes behave under addition: most notably, the sum of a positive and a negative number can be positive, zero, or negative.
          %p
            %strong Proposition
          %p
            To each element,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ,
                  %mi 𝕊
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(\mathbb{Z}[X], \mathbb{S})
            there corresponds an extended real number,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Re
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo ,
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Re(\phi) \in [-\infty, \infty]
            given as a Dedekind cut. This is a surjective mapping. The inverse image of each real algebraic number contains three elements, while that of a nonalgebraic number is a singleton. For real algebraic
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}[X]
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕊
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{S}
            are
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↦
                  %munder
                    %mi lim
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mo →
                      %mn 0
                      %mo +
                  %mi sign
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi α
                  %mo +
                  %mi t
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi t
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  P(T) \mapsto \underset{\epsilon \to 0+} {lim} sign P(\alpha + t \epsilon), t \in \{-1, 0, 1\}.
          %h3#the_tropical_hyperfield_
            The tropical hyperfield
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕋
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{T}
          %p
            The
            %em tropical hyperfield
            has as its underlying set
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕋
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{T} = [0,\infty]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi 𝕋
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a, b \in \mathbb{T}
            is defined to be the usual
            %em sum
            of nonnegative numbers for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a, b\in [0,\infty)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo =
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \cdot \infty = \infty
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi 𝕋
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a,b \in \mathbb{T}
            is defined to be
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mi max
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \max b
            when
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ≠
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \ne b
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a = b
            it is defined to be the set
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,a]
          %p
            %strong Proposition.
            For any commutative ring
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi R
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝕋
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \colon R \to \mathbb{T}
            are the same as nonarchimedean valuations
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi R
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \colon R \to [0,\infty]
          %p
            This was observed first by
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Viro10"} Viro (2010)
            = succeed "," do
              %em Tropical geometry over the tropical hyperfield
          %h3#the_triangle_hyperfield_
            The triangle hyperfield
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
          %p
            The
            %em triangle hyperfield
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            is the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo +
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^+ = [0,\infty)
            with its usual multiplication, where the sum of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            is the set of all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \in \mathbb{R}^+
            such
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a,b,c
            are the lengths of the sides of a triangle in the plane (allowing sides of length zero). Viro showed
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            is a hyperfield in Theorem 5.4 of his paper on hyperfields in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tropical+geometry"} tropical geometry
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 3
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 3
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 9
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (2 + 1) \cdot (2 + 1) = [1,3] \cdot [1,3] = [1,9]
          %p while
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %mn 4
                  %mo +
                  %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 9
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 \cdot 2 + 2 \cdot 1 + 1 \cdot 2 + 1 \cdot 1 = 4 + 2 + 2 + 1 = [0,9]
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hypermagma"} hypermagma
